### What does KafkaTemplate provide over the raw producer?

With the raw client you must construct a `KafkaProducer`, call `send`, deal with the returned `Future`, flush, and close it yourself. Spring Kafka gives you `KafkaTemplate`, which wraps one shared producer and is fully thread-safe, so every thread in the application can call it.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.kafka.support.SendResult;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;

@Service
public class TransferEventPublisher {
    private final KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate;

    public TransferEventPublisher(KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate) {
        this.kafkaTemplate = kafkaTemplate;
    }

    public void publishTransfer(String accountId, String event) {
        CompletableFuture<SendResult<String, String>> future =
            kafkaTemplate.send("transfer.completed", accountId, event);

        future.whenComplete((result, ex) -> {
            if (ex == null) {
                System.out.println("Sent to partition "
                    + result.getRecordMetadata().partition());
            } else {
                System.err.println("Send failed: " + ex.getMessage());
            }
        });
    }
}
```

```text
Sent to partition 3
```

### What does @KafkaListener provide over the raw consumer?

The raw client forces you to write an infinite poll loop with manual deserialization, offset commits, and shutdown handling. Spring Kafka replaces all of that with a declarative annotation. The container deserializes each record, invokes your method, commits offsets, catches exceptions, and manages start and stop.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class SettlementListener {

    @KafkaListener(topics = "transfer.completed", groupId = "settlement-group")
    public void onTransfer(String event) {
        System.out.println("Settling: " + event);
        // Business logic only -- container handles poll, commit, errors
    }
}
```

```text
Settling: {"accountId":"SAV2001","amount":50000}
```

### How does the concurrency setting map threads to partitions?

Setting `factory.setConcurrency(3)` creates three child containers, each with its own consumer, all in the same consumer group. Kafka's rebalance distributes partitions across them. The rule is that a single partition is only ever assigned to one consumer in the group at a time.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.kafka.config.ConcurrentKafkaListenerContainerFactory;
import org.springframework.kafka.core.ConsumerFactory;
import org.springframework.kafka.core.DefaultKafkaConsumerFactory;
import java.util.Map;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerConfig;

@Configuration
public class KafkaConsumerConfig {

    @Bean
    public ConcurrentKafkaListenerContainerFactory<String, String> kafkaListenerContainerFactory() {
        ConcurrentKafkaListenerContainerFactory<String, String> factory =
            new ConcurrentKafkaListenerContainerFactory<>();
        factory.setConsumerFactory(consumerFactory());
        factory.setConcurrency(3); // 3 consumer threads
        return factory;
    }

    @Bean
    public ConsumerFactory<String, String> consumerFactory() {
        return new DefaultKafkaConsumerFactory<>(Map.of(
            ConsumerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "kafka-1:9092",
            ConsumerConfig.KEY_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringDeserializer.class,
            ConsumerConfig.VALUE_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringDeserializer.class
        ));
    }
}
```

```text
# Topic with 12 partitions, concurrency=3:
# Thread-0 owns partitions [0, 1, 2, 3]
# Thread-1 owns partitions [4, 5, 6, 7]
# Thread-2 owns partitions [8, 9, 10, 11]

# Topic with 2 partitions, concurrency=3:
# Thread-0 owns partition [0]
# Thread-1 owns partition [1]
# Thread-2 is IDLE (no partition to assign)
```

### Why must you not create a KafkaTemplate per request?

A Kafka producer is a heavy object that batches and compresses across threads for throughput. Creating one per request wastes resources and prevents batching. KafkaTemplate is designed to be shared.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

// WRONG: creating a new producer per request
public class BadProducerUsage {
    public void send(String event) {
        // KafkaTemplate template = new KafkaTemplate<>(new DefaultKafkaProducerFactory<>(...));
        // template.send("topic", event);
        // This creates a new producer every call -- expensive, no batching!
        System.out.println("WRONG: new producer per request");
    }
}

// CORRECT: inject the shared KafkaTemplate
// @Service
// public class GoodProducerUsage {
//     private final KafkaTemplate<String, String> template; // injected, shared
//     public void send(String event) { template.send("topic", event); }
// }
```

```text
WRONG: new producer per request
```

### How does keying preserve ordering with concurrency?

Keying by account ID routes all of Rajesh's events to the same partition. Since one partition is assigned to one thread, all of Rajesh's events are processed in order by a single thread, even with high concurrency.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class OrderPreservingPublisher {
    private final KafkaTemplate<String, String> template;

    public OrderPreservingPublisher(KafkaTemplate<String, String> template) {
        this.template = template;
    }

    public void publish(String accountId, String event) {
        // Key = accountId: all events for SAV2001 go to the same partition
        template.send("transfer.completed", accountId, event);
        System.out.println("Event for " + accountId + " routed to consistent partition");
    }
}
```

```text
Event for SAV2001 routed to consistent partition
```

### How do you configure Spring Kafka with application.properties?

Spring Boot auto-configures most settings from properties, so you often do not need a manual configuration class.

```properties
# application.properties
spring.kafka.bootstrap-servers=kafka-1:9092
spring.kafka.producer.key-serializer=org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer
spring.kafka.producer.value-serializer=org.springframework.kafka.support.serializer.JsonSerializer
spring.kafka.consumer.key-deserializer=org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer
spring.kafka.consumer.value-deserializer=org.springframework.kafka.support.serializer.JsonDeserializer
spring.kafka.consumer.group-id=settlement-group
spring.kafka.consumer.auto-offset-reset=earliest
spring.kafka.listener.concurrency=3
```

### How do you scale across multiple application instances?

The concurrency number is per instance. If you run three instances each with concurrency=4, that is twelve consumers in the group. Kafka distributes the topic's partitions across all twelve. Total parallelism is still capped by partition count across the whole group.

```text
# Topic: transfer.completed (12 partitions)
# Instance-1 (concurrency=4): threads own partitions [0,1,2,3]
# Instance-2 (concurrency=4): threads own partitions [4,5,6,7]
# Instance-3 (concurrency=4): threads own partitions [8,9,10,11]
# Perfect utilization: 12 threads for 12 partitions

# If you add Instance-4 (concurrency=4):
# 16 threads but only 12 partitions -- 4 threads idle
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Choose a partition count that matches peak desired parallelism, then set concurrency per instance so instances times concurrency does not exceed partitions. KafkaTemplate being one shared producer is intentional because producers batch across threads. Ordering per partition is the foundation of per-account ordering, which is critical in banking.

```bash
# Sizing rule of thumb
# Peak parallelism needed: 12
# Create topic with 12 partitions
# Deploy 3 instances with concurrency=4 each (3 x 4 = 12)
# Or deploy 4 instances with concurrency=3 each (4 x 3 = 12)
```
